<h2>Why Commercial Roofs in Austin Age Faster</h2>
<p>A flat roof in Central Texas lives a hard life. Surface temperatures on a dark membrane can run far above the air temperature on a summer afternoon, and the daily swing from a cool night to a hot afternoon works every seam and fastener. Spring brings hail that can bruise bitumen and puncture membranes, and straight-line winds that peel back poorly secured edges and copings. Live oak and cedar debris clogs drains on buildings near trees, and the expansive clay soils around Austin can shift a structure enough to stress parapet walls and flashings over time.</p>
<p>That is why we recommend at least two inspections a year — once in early spring before hail season and once in fall after the summer heat — plus a look after any major storm. A maintenance plan that catches a split seam early costs a fraction of replacing saturated insulation later.</p>

<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Roof still solid but showing its age?</strong> A <a href="/services/commercial-roof-coatings/">commercial roof coating</a> can seal minor leaks and add reflectivity without a full tear-off — if the membrane and insulation underneath are still in good shape.</p></div>

<h2>Storm Damage and Insurance on Commercial Roofs</h2>
<p>After hail or high winds, we inspect and photograph the roof, mark damage, and prepare documentation your insurance carrier or property manager can use. We can meet the adjuster on site to walk the damage. We are roofers, not public adjusters, so coverage decisions stay between you and your insurer — but a clear, well-documented report makes that conversation easier. Our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claims/">roof insurance claim help</a> page explains what we do and do not do.</p>

